 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time varies significantly by method and hardware. Generalizable NeRF modules take about 2 days [S10]. Fine-tuning ranges from 30 to 90 minutes depending on video frames [S10]. Neural appearance refinement takes 1-2 days [S10]. Some methods converge in ~15 minutes per scene [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on representation (voxel grid size, hash table entries, particle count). Mixed precision training reduces memory bandwidth requirements .
-*   **Hardware:** Commonly uses NVIDIA GPUs (V100, A100, RTX 3090, A5000, RTX 6000 Ada) [S1, S7, S8, S9, S10, S11, S13].
-*   **Strengths:** Configurable hyperparameters allow balancing between convergence speed, visual quality, and computational cost.
-*   **Limitations:** Poorly chosen hyperparameters can lead to overfitting, slow convergence, or artifacts. Sparse-view scenarios are particularly sensitive to configuration [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of training iterations and batch size. Convergence speed varies by method (e.g., 15 minutes for Direct Voxel Grid [S2] vs. hours for complex dynamic scenes [S12]).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of model parameters and batch size. Mixed precision [2766-mixed-precision-training.md] reduces memory bandwidth requirements.
+*   **Performance:** Measured in PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS. High-resolution training (e.g., 200k iterations) yields higher quality but requires more time [S8, S13].
+*   **Strengths:** Flexible configuration allows tuning for specific datasets (static, dynamic, sparse-view).
+*   **Limitations:** Hyperparameter sensitivity; poor configuration can lead to divergence or overfitting.
+*   **When to Use:** Standard for all NeRF training. Specific configurations (e.g., Dynamic Frequency Masking) for few-shot or high-frequency detail enhancement [S15, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Training configuration is a fundamental aspect of neural radiance field optimization, evolving from the original NeRF implementation [37] to include specialized schedules for various representations (voxel grids, hash tables, Gaussian splats). Key developments include the introduction of dynamic frequency masking in FreeNeRF [4] and Few TensoRF [S15, unofficial], and adaptive sampling strategies in methods like Zip-NeRF [S3] and MERF [S6].
+Training configuration has evolved from the original NeRF paper [37] which used a simple Adam optimizer with a fixed learning rate. Subsequent works introduced adaptive learning rates [S2], mixed precision [S1], and method-specific configurations for dynamic scenes [S12], sparse views [S15, unofficial], and object detection [S16]. The field has moved towards more sophisticated scheduling and regularization to handle complex scenes and limited data.
 
